WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.181
Hi, I'm Kelvin!

00:00:02.181 --> 00:00:05.450
Liquid's new decision model says that when it is ninety percent

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:06.638
sure, it is right.

00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:09.609
Let's see what that number is, and where it holds.

00:00:10.892 --> 00:00:14.218
A decision model scores every option and returns probabilities.

00:00:14.218 --> 00:00:19.023
If they are calibrated, a threshold can decide what runs without a person.

00:00:20.883 --> 00:00:22.658
The state and a question go in.

00:00:22.658 --> 00:00:25.701
Each question is its own prompt, and it is billed as one.

00:00:26.121 --> 00:00:29.552
One scoring pass reads a score for every option.

00:00:29.552 --> 00:00:31.840
A softmax turns them into probabilities.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.984
Nothing is generated.

00:00:33.404 --> 00:00:37.236
Take the probability of the chosen option and compare it with a threshold.

00:00:37.656 --> 00:00:39.864
Above it, the decision runs on its own.

00:00:39.864 --> 00:00:41.245
Below it, a person looks.

00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:44.399
Calibration has a number.

00:00:45.025 --> 00:00:46.707
Sort answers into bins by confidence.

00:00:46.707 --> 00:00:50.070
In each bin, compare how often it was right with how sure

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:51.191
it said it was.

00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:54.037
Weight each gap by the share of answers in that bin,

00:00:54.037 --> 00:00:54.920
and add them up.

00:00:56.317 --> 00:01:01.335
Pool four benchmarks into one curve, and errors that point in opposite directions cancel.

00:01:01.755 --> 00:01:05.548
Split them, and d1-ultra is cautious on tool calls and safety, and

00:01:05.548 --> 00:01:08.392
sure of itself on exam questions it gets wrong.

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:13.987
On the pooled curve, d1-ultra's error is three point six, a third

00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:15.272
of the next model's.

00:01:15.272 --> 00:01:16.878
Per benchmark, it ranks fourth.

00:01:18.508 --> 00:01:21.222
Its index score recomputes to sixty five point two five.

00:01:21.222 --> 00:01:25.022
Most of the lead comes from saying I don't know when it does not.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:30.249
A threshold is only as good as the calibration on your own task.

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:32.087
Measure it per task, never pooled.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.159
Score the options, trust a threshold only where it is calibrated, and never pool.

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:41.689
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.689 --> 00:01:42.291
I'm Kelvin.

00:01:42.291 --> 00:01:42.593
Bye!
